    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p82-q59-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that none of the cubic functions will have a positive solution.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "None has a positive zero.\n\nFor $x>0$, every term in each function is positive.",
          "worked": "A positive solution would be a zero with $x>0$. Since $a>0$ and $b>0$, every term below is positive when $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=x^3+ax^2+bx>0\\\\g(x)&=ax^3+x^2+bx>0\\\\h(x)&=ax^3+bx^2+x>0\\end{align*}$$\nNone can equal zero for $x>0$, so none has a positive zero.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p82-q59-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that it is impossible for all three cubic functions to have three distinct zeroes simultaneously.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "It is impossible.\n\nThree distinct zeroes require $a^2>4b$, $1>4ab$ and $b^2>4a$. Multiplication gives $a^2b^2>64a^2b^2$, contradicting $a^2b^2>0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=x(x^2+ax+b)\\\\g(x)&=x(ax^2+x+b)\\\\h(x)&=x(ax^2+bx+1)\\end{align*}$$\nEach cubic already has the zero $0$. Since $a,b>0$, the remaining factors are quadratics with nonzero constant terms. Their roots therefore cannot be $0$.\n\nFor all three cubics to have three distinct zeroes, each quadratic must have two distinct real roots:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta_f&=a^2-4b>0\\\\\\Delta_g&=1-4ab>0\\\\\\Delta_h&=b^2-4a>0\\end{align*}$$\nThus $a^2>4b$, $1>4ab$ and $b^2>4a$. All quantities being multiplied are positive, so\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2b^2&>(4b)(4ab)(4a)\\\\a^2b^2&>64a^2b^2\\end{align*}$$\nDividing by $a^2b^2>0$ gives $1>64$, a contradiction. Hence all three cubics cannot have three distinct zeroes simultaneously.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      },
      {
        "id": "p82-q59-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p82-q59-c-prompt",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Suppose instead that ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "a",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " and ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "b",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " are ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "negative",
                  "marks": [
                    "italic"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ". Is it possible for all three cubic functions to have three distinct zeroes simultaneously?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Yes, exactly when $ab<\\frac14$, with $a,b<0$.\n\nThe discriminants for $f$ and $h$ are automatically positive; the remaining requirement is $1-4ab>0$.",
          "worked": "Now $a<0$ and $b<0$. Using the quadratic factors from part (b),\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta_f&=a^2-4b>0\\\\\\Delta_h&=b^2-4a>0\\end{align*}$$\nThese inequalities hold automatically. The remaining quadratic has two distinct real roots when\n$$\\begin{align*}1-4ab&>0\\\\ab&<\\frac14\\end{align*}$$\nAll three quadratic constant terms are nonzero, so none of their roots coincides with the existing zero $0$.\n\nTherefore, yes: all three cubics have three distinct zeroes exactly when $ab<\\frac14$. For example, $a=b=-\\frac14$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}ab&=\\left(-\\frac14\\right)\\left(-\\frac14\\right)\\\\ab&=\\frac1{16}<\\frac14\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
